How the explanation develops
Begin with a recognizable problem. Tasha’s POV overview starts from a question writers have been asking her. Her preparation article identifies who the method is for and why she needs it herself. The opening gives the reader a reason to continue before asking them to absorb a framework.
Define enough to make the next point understandable. Use the term, explain it plainly, then show what it does in practice. Tasha is comfortable with craft vocabulary, but her explanation keeps returning to a scene, a reader’s experience, or an action the writer can take.
Connect advice to its consequence. Explain what becomes confusing, what information a narrator cannot know, what interrupts a reader’s connection, or what sends the writer wandering away from the manuscript. A rule becomes more persuasive when the reader can see the failure it addresses.
Give a specific demonstration. Use a short passage, a concrete scenario, an observable comparison, or a procedure someone can follow. The selfie exercise is especially useful evidence: it turns a discussion of perspective into something the reader can actually try.
Include the relevant exception. The filler-word article moves from identifying clutter to situations where a character’s speech or narrative audience makes the same words useful. Preserve that willingness to qualify advice. When evidence only supports a tendency, use wording appropriate to a tendency.
Finish with application. An exercise, a revision decision, or a practical next step suits this voice. The reader should leave knowing what to look for or what to do. Avoid appending a motivational conclusion after the useful ending has already happened.
These movements are options, not mandatory section headings. A short explanation may need only a problem, an example, and a fix. A chapter can develop the same reasoning across several pages without repeating the structure mechanically.
The relationship with the reader
Use “you” when the reader is doing something and “I” when Tasha is describing her own practice, experience, or judgment. “We” can acknowledge a shared writing habit when that inclusion is earned. Keep personal experience bounded: a method working for Tasha is a reason to explain and test it, not automatic proof that it works for everyone.
Assume the reader can understand the subject once it is explained. Give clear directions without wrapping each one in encouragement or qualification. Where a question appears, it should direct attention to a real choice, expose a problem, or invite the reader to test an example.
Self-disclosure should help the reader. Admitting a distracting habit can explain why a preparation method exists. A personal anecdote should lead somewhere relevant. Do not invent a client, a conversation, a test result, or a lived experience to manufacture this quality.
